    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the membership-level ceiling rule from the spec: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\n### Changes to `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`\n\n**1. The membership-level cap (the core spec).** The controller's `membershipLevel(Owner)` is the codebase's designated single place where cross-owner household context shapes the reported level (its own javadoc says so, and both the create audit log and the single-owner read route through it). I made it:\n- start from the owner-local level (`ownerMapper.membershipLevel`),\n- compute the max owner-local level among the owner's **existing** household members (same `householdId`, excluding the owner itself by id),\n- return `min(ownLevel, maxHouseholdLevel + 1)`, or the uncapped level when there are no existing household members.\n\nA helper `householdMemberLevel(Owner)` derives each member's own level (setting its household size first, as the report path does). The cap intentionally uses members' **owner-local** levels, not their capped levels, so it can't recurse.\n\n**2. A required enabling fix to `rejectHouseholdDuplicate`.** The new test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName+postcode) with **different telephones** and expects both to succeed \u2014 but the old rule hard-rejected *every* second same-household create with a 409, so the level cap could never be observed. That rule was also inconsistent with the existing `possibleDuplicateOf` soft-match (same lastName+postcode, *different* telephone), which was unreachable dead code because of it. I scoped the household-duplicate check to reject only when an existing member shares the same canonical **telephone** (a genuine near-duplicate); distinct-telephone members are now allowed and surfaced as `possibleDuplicate`, exactly as the test's \"unique telephone\" comment and the soft-match design intend.\n\nNote: this was a black-box acceptance suite with a single test, and it and all main/test sources compile and pass. The second change touches a rule beyond the literal one-sentence spec, but the test cannot go green without allowing distinct household members \u2014 flag it if you'd prefer the household rule left untouched and the test adjusted instead.",
